\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Ch2#10
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Theorem
\emph default
.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is periodic with period 
\begin_inset Formula $2\pi$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 times continuously differentiable.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=O\left(\frac{1}{\left|k\right|^{m}}\right)$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|k\right|\to\infty$
\end_inset

,
 which is to say that there exists a constant 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\fhat(k)\right|\le\frac{C}{\left|k\right|^{m}}$
\end_inset

,
 which is to say that there exists a bound 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|k\right|^{m}\left|\fhat(k)\right|\le B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Comment
\emph default
.
 We get a hint to integrate by parts (which is now legal,
 since we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is differentiable (
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 times continuously).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 Assigning 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\underbrace{f(\theta)}_{u}\underbrace{\exp(-ik\theta)}_{dv}\,d\theta$
\end_inset

 yields 
\begin_inset Formula $du:=f'(\theta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v:=\frac{-1}{ik}\exp(-ik\theta)$
\end_inset

,
 such that we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\left.\frac{-1}{ik}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\right|_{\theta:=-\pi}^{\pi}+\frac{1}{ik}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $=\frac{-1}{ik}f(\pi)\exp(-ik\pi)+\frac{1}{ik}f(-\pi)\exp(ik\pi)+\frac{1}{ik}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is periodic with period 
\begin_inset Formula $2\pi$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(-\pi)=f(\pi)$
\end_inset

,
 so the boundary terms become 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{i}{k}f(\pi)\exp(-ik\pi)-\frac{i}{k}f(\pi)\exp(ik\pi)=f(\pi)\frac{i}{k}\cdot\left(\exp(-ik\pi)-\exp(ik\pi)\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So far we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\cdot\frac{i}{k}\cdot\left(f(\pi)\cdot\left(\exp(-ik\pi)-\exp(ik\pi)\right)-\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta\right)$
\end_inset

.
 And then,
 what,
 we integrate by parts again (we can go to 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{(m)}$
\end_inset

),
 and get another factor of 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{i}{k}$
\end_inset

?
 But that wouldn't affect the boundary terms from the first integration-by-parts.
 Halp?!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Claude Opus 5 offers the hint that we can do more to evaluate 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{i}{k}\cdot f(\pi)\cdot\left(\exp(-ik\pi)-\exp(ik\pi)\right)$
\end_inset

 at successive integers.
 That is,
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k:=1$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-\pi i}-e^{\pi i}=-1-(-1)=0$
\end_inset

.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $k:=2$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-2\pi i}-e^{2\pi i}=1-1=0$
\end_inset

.
 The difference 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 half-turns forward and backward will always be zero,
 so the boundary term does cancel.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That gives us 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=-\frac{1}{2\pi}\cdot\frac{i}{k}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta$
\end_inset

 (Opus 5 pointed out that I forgot the factor of 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{ik}$
\end_inset

 here),
 which is to say that 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\frac{1}{ik}\hat{f'}(k)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But then integrating 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f'}(k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\underbrace{f'(\theta)}_{u}\underbrace{\exp(-ik\theta)}_{dv}\,d\theta$
\end_inset

 is going to go the same:
 it's going to be 
\begin_inset Formula $\left.\frac{-1}{ik}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\right|_{\theta:=-\pi}^{\pi}+\frac{1}{ik}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f''(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta$
\end_inset

.
 The boundary term is going to vanish again.
 (A periodic function has a periodic derivative.) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So it would seem that 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\frac{1}{ik}\hat{f'}(k)=-\frac{1}{k^{2}}\hat{f''}(k)$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
&c
\emph default
.
 But derivatives only came into it because we used parts;
 we're actually trying to bound 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\fhat(k)\right|$
\end_inset

.
 We have 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\fhat(k)\right|=\frac{1}{\left|k\right|^{m}}\left|\hat{f^{(m)}(k)}\right|$
\end_inset

 (and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f^{(m)}}$
\end_inset

 has to exist because the function is 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 times continuously differentiable).
 So the qualitative moral here is that if a function is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

-any
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 times continuously differentiable,
 then the Fourier coëfficients have to go to zero like an 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

th power,
 which means that for smooth functions,
 it's decreasing faster than any fixed power.
 So smooth functions can't have too much weight on sufficiently high-frequency components?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Opus 5 points out that we didn't finish getting a bound:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\hat{f^{(m)}(k)}\right|$
\end_inset

 isn't constant in 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
 But if 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k}\hat{f^{(m)}}\exp(ik\theta)$
\end_inset

 converges,
 then its coëfficients have to go to zero,
 so there will be a bound.
 Opus 5 objects that we don't know that the series converges (this point in the curriculum is doing a lot of work on that question!) and gets us started on the actual bound:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\hat{f^{(m)}(k)}\right|=\left|\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f^{(m)}(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta\right|\le\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\left|f^{(m)}(\theta)\right|\underbrace{\left|\exp(-ik\theta)\right|}_{=1}\,d\theta\le\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\left|f^{(m)}(\theta)\right|\,d\theta$
\end_inset

 but since 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{(m)}$
\end_inset

 iis continuous on the compact interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[-\pi,\pi]$
\end_inset

,
 it must be bounded by the extreme value theorem.
