\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
§3.6.1#1
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 The following statements are equivalent in a real pre-Hilbert space.
 (i) 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 are orthogonal.
 (ii) 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert x+y\right\Vert ^{2}=\left\Vert x\right\Vert ^{2}+\left\Vert y\right\Vert ^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 (iii) For all 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\in\R$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert x\right\Vert \le\left\Vert x+\lambda y\right\Vert $
\end_inset

.
 (iv) 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert x+y\right\Vert =\left\Vert x-y\right\Vert $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 (i 
\begin_inset Formula $\Rightarrow$
\end_inset

ii) Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle x,y\right\rangle =0$
\end_inset

,
 and recall that the metric is defined such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert x\right\Vert =\sqrt{\left\langle x,x\right\rangle }$
\end_inset

.
 So 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert x+y\right\Vert ^{2}=\left\langle x+y,x+y\right\rangle =\left\langle x,x+y\right\rangle +\left\langle y,x+y\right\rangle =\cancel{\left\langle x,y\right\rangle }+\left\langle x,x\right\rangle +\cancel{\left\langle y,x\right\rangle }+\left\langle y,y\right\rangle =\left\Vert x\right\Vert ^{2}+\left\Vert y\right\Vert ^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(ii
\begin_inset Formula $\Rightarrow$
\end_inset

i) By the same algebra,
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert x+y\right\Vert ^{2}=\left\Vert x\right\Vert ^{2}+\left\Vert y\right\Vert ^{2}+\left\langle x,y\right\rangle +\left\langle y,x\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 so if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert x+y\right\Vert ^{2}=\left\Vert x\right\Vert ^{2}+\left\Vert y\right\Vert ^{2}$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle x,y\right\rangle +\left\langle y,x\right\rangle =0$
\end_inset

.
 But over 
\begin_inset Formula $\R$
\end_inset

 we have symmetry 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle x,y\right\rangle =\left\langle y,x\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 so we have 
\begin_inset Formula $2\left\langle x,y\right\rangle =0$
\end_inset

 and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle x,y\right\rangle =0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(i
\begin_inset Formula $\Rightarrow$
\end_inset

iii) 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert x\right\Vert ^{2}=\left\langle x,x\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 but 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert x+\lambda y\right\Vert ^{2}=\left\langle x+\lambda y,x+\lambda y\right\rangle =\left\langle x+\lambda y,x\right\rangle +\left\langle x+\lambda y,\lambda y\right\rangle =\left\langle x,x\right\rangle +\cancel{\left\langle \lambda y,x\right\rangle }+\cancel{\left\langle x,\lambda y\right\rangle }+\left\langle \lambda y,\lambda y\right\rangle \ge\left\langle x,x\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(iii
\begin_inset Formula $\Rightarrow$
\end_inset

i) Same algebra,
 but we observe that in 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle x,x\right\rangle +\left\langle \lambda y,x\right\rangle +\left\langle x,\lambda y\right\rangle +\left\langle \lambda y,\lambda y\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 the self-product terms are nonnegative.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle x,\lambda y\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 isn't zero,
 we could make it negative with an appropriate choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 to violate the inequality,
 so it must be zero.
 Claude Opus 5 points out a gap:
 we have to show not only that 
\begin_inset Formula $2\left\langle x,\lambda y\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 could be negative,
 but that it's bigger than 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle \lambda y,\lambda y\right\rangle $
\end_inset

;
 the answer is that for small 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

,
 the linear-in-
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 cross-term is bigger than than the quadratic 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda y$
\end_inset

 term.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(iv
\begin_inset Formula $\Rightarrow$
\end_inset

i) 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle x+y,x+y\right\rangle =\left\langle x,x\right\rangle +2\left\langle x,y\right\rangle +\left\langle y,y\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 but 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle x-y,x-y\right\rangle =\left\langle x-y,x\right\rangle -\left\langle x-y,y\right\rangle =\left\langle x,x\right\rangle -\left\langle y,x\right\rangle -\left(\left\langle x,y\right\rangle -\left\langle y,y\right\rangle \right)=\left\langle x,x\right\rangle -2\left\langle y,x\right\rangle +\left\langle y,y\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $2\left\langle x,y\right\rangle =-2\left\langle x,y\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 which implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle x,y\right\rangle =0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

