\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
§3.6.1#8
\series default
.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 be the pre-Hilbert space of functions 
\begin_inset Formula $f:[0,1]\to\C$
\end_inset

 with inner product 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle f,g\right\rangle =\int_{0}^{1}f(x)\overline{g(x)}\,dx.$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 The subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 of functions such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}}f(x)\,dx=\int_{\frac{1}{2}}^{1}f(x)\,dx$
\end_inset

 is closed in 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 Closed sets contain their limits.
 Consider an arbitrary sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{k}$
\end_inset

 in M,
 which means that for every 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}}f_{k}(x)\,dx=\int_{\frac{1}{2}}^{1}f_{k}(x)\,dx$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Claude Opus 5 suggests we proceed by talking about the kernel of a linear functional.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $T(f):=\int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}}f-\int_{\frac{1}{2}}^{1}f$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is continuous with respect to our norm,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{k}$
\end_inset

 converging to 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $T(f_{k})$
\end_inset

 converges to 
\begin_inset Formula $T(f)$
\end_inset

.
 We know that 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{k}$
\end_inset

 converges to 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 by hypothesis,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $T(f_{k})$
\end_inset

 converges to 
\begin_inset Formula $T(f)$
\end_inset

.
 For all 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $T(f_{k})=0$
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\{T(f_{k})\}_{k}$
\end_inset

 must converge to 0,
 so by the uniqueness of limits,
 
\begin_inset Formula $T(f)$
\end_inset

 must be zero,
 which means that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is in 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It remains to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is continuous with respect to the norm,
 which is to say that it's bounded.
 Opus 5 says to use linearity (and corrects me being retarded about norms).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|T(f)-T(g)\right|=\left|T(f-g)\right|=\left|\int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}}(f-g)-\int_{\frac{1}{2}}^{1}(f-g)\right|\le\left|\int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}}(f-g)\right|+\left|\int_{\frac{1}{2}}^{1}(f-g)\right|\le\int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}}\left|f-g\right|+\int_{\frac{1}{2}}^{1}\left|f-g\right|=\int_{0}^{1}\left|f-g\right|$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then there's a hint to apply Cauchy–Schwarz to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|f-g\right|$
\end_inset

 and the constant function 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

.
 I guess that would be 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\left\langle \left|f-g\right|,1\right\rangle \right|\le\left\Vert f-g\right\Vert \left\Vert 1\right\Vert $
\end_inset

?
 I should confess that
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle \left|f-g\right|,1\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 does not feel meaningful to me.
 (I'm used to the concrete dot product in 
\begin_inset Formula $\R^{n}$
\end_inset

,
 which does not clearly have a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

—

\begin_inset Formula $\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0\\
0
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset

 is not the same thing.) But if I just go with it,
 presumably 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle f-g,1\right\rangle =f-g$
\end_inset

?—
no,
 because first of all,
 that doesn't typecheck (the inner product is a scalar),
 but more importantly,
 I don't have to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

just go with it
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 because we have a definition:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle \left|f-g\right|,1\right\rangle =\int_{0}^{1}\left|f-g\right|\cdot\overline{1}\,dx=\int_{0}^{1}\left|f-g\right|$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So the Cauchy–Schwarz-with-one gambit gives us 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\int_{0}^{1}\left|f-g\right|\right|=\int_{0}^{1}\left|f-g\right|\le\left\Vert f-g\right\Vert $
\end_inset

.
 Putting it together,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|T(f)-T(g)\right|\le\int_{0}^{1}\left|f-g\right|\le\left\Vert f-g\right\Vert $
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is bounded,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is continuous.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Dirichlet's test relies on summation by parts,
 which is the discrete analogue of integration by parts,
 which a lot of retarded people just memorize by rote (
\begin_inset Formula $\int u\,dv=uv-\int v\,du$
\end_inset

) without understanding that it's a corollary of the product rule:
 
\begin_inset Formula $(uv)^{\prime}=u^{\prime}v+uv^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 integrates to 
\begin_inset Formula $\int(uv)^{\prime}=\int u^{\prime}v+\int uv^{\prime}$
\end_inset

,
 the fundamental theorem converts the left side to 
\begin_inset Formula $uv$
\end_inset

,
 so we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\int uv^{\prime}=uv-\int u^{\prime}v$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For summation by parts,
 we would start with a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

discrete product rule
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 For a sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\{a_{k}\}_{k}$
\end_inset

,
 define the difference delta operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta a_{n}:=a_{n+1}-a_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(a_{n}b_{n})=a_{n+1}b_{n+1}-a_{n}b_{n}=a_{n+1}b_{n+1}-a_{n}b_{n}+\underbrace{a_{n}b_{n+1}-a_{n}b_{n+1}}_{\mathrm{``fancy\,zero"}}=b_{n+1}(a_{n+1}-a_{n})+a_{n}(b_{n+1}-b_{n})=\Delta(a_{n})b_{n+1}+a_{n}\Delta(b_{n})$
\end_inset

.
 (The asymmetry with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{n}$
\end_inset

 but 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{n+1}$
\end_inset

 is real;
 the symmetric version would have a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

quadratic
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 term which vanishes in the limit in the continuous version.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fundamental theorem analogue is a telescoping sum:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{k=M}^{N}\Delta(a_{k}b_{k})=\underbrace{\left(\cancel{a_{M+1}b_{M+1}}-a_{M}b_{M}\right)}_{\Delta(a_{M}b_{M})}+\underbrace{\left(\cancel{a_{M+2}b_{M+2}}-\cancel{a_{M+1}b_{M+1}}\right)}_{\Delta(a_{M+1}b_{M+1})}+...+\underbrace{\left(a_{N+1}b_{N+1}\cancel{-a_{N}b_{N}}\right)}_{\Delta(a_{N}b_{N})}=a_{N+1}b_{N+1}-a_{M}b_{M}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(a_{n}b_{n})=\Delta(a_{n})b_{n+1}+a_{n}\Delta(b_{n})$
\end_inset

 gets summed on both sides to become 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{N+1}b_{N+1}-a_{M}b_{M}=\sum_{k=M}^{N}\Delta(a_{k})b_{k+1}+\sum_{k=M}^{N}a_{k}\Delta(b_{k})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But the sequence I've called 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{k}$
\end_inset

 here might be a sequence of partial sums of some other sequence,
 which I'll call 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 That is 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{k}:=\sum_{j=1}^{k}c_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Then we have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a_{N+1}\sum_{j=1}^{N+1}c_{j}-a_{M}\sum_{j=1}^{M}c_{j}=\sum_{k=M}^{N}\Delta(a_{k})\sum_{j=1}^{k+1}c_{j}+\sum_{k=M}^{N}a_{k}c_{k+1}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which is so unreadable that we'll reluctantly defer to tradition (I wanted to see the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

sum-ness
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) and write 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{k}:=\sum_{j=1}^{k}c_{j}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{k=M}^{N}a_{k}c_{k+1}=a_{N+1}B_{N+1}-a_{M}B_{M}-\sum_{k=M}^{N}\Delta(a_{k})B_{k+1}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The reason we're bothering with this is that we want Dirichlet's test.
 If some conditions on 
\begin_inset Formula $\{a_{k}\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{c_{k}\}$
\end_inset

 let us make 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\sum_{k=M}^{N}a_{k}c_{k}\right|$
\end_inset

 small for sufficiently large 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}a_{k}c_{k}$
\end_inset

 will converge by the Cauchy criterion for series.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose the partial sums of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}c_{k}$
\end_inset

 are bounded in magnitude,
 say by 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 (this is weaker than converges because the partial sums could bounce around without converging) and that 
\begin_inset Formula $\{a_{k}\}\downarrow0$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\sum_{k=M}^{N}a_{k}c_{k+1}\right|=\left|a_{N+1}B_{N+1}-a_{M}B_{M}-\sum_{k=M}^{N}\Delta(a_{k})B_{k+1}\right|\le\left|a_{N+1}B_{N+1}\right|+\left|a_{M}B_{M}\right|+\left|\sum_{k=M}^{N}\Delta(a_{k})B_{k+1}\right|
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\le\left|a_{N+1}B_{N+1}\right|+\left|a_{M}B_{M}\right|+\sum_{k=M}^{N}\left|\Delta(a_{k})\right|\left|B_{k+1}\right|\le C\cdot\left(\left|a_{N+1}\right|+\left|a_{M}\right|+\sum_{k=M}^{N}\left|\Delta(a_{k})\right|\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But since 
\begin_inset Formula $\{a_{k}\}$
\end_inset

 is decreasing monotonically to zero,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=M}^{N}\left|\Delta(a_{k})\right|\le(N-M)\cdot a_{M}$
\end_inset

,
 and for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

,
 there exists a 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

such that if 
\begin_inset Formula $k\ge K$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{k}<\frac{\varepsilon}{\left((M-N)+2\right)C}$
\end_inset

.
 For for 
\begin_inset Formula $N>M\ge K$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=M}^{N}a_{k}c_{k+1}\le\cancel{C}\cdot\cancel{\left((M-N)+2\right)}\cdot\frac{\varepsilon}{\cancel{\left((M-N)+2\right)}\cancel{C}}=\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

